@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+# SLO
+
+Service level objectives for the single-host certified stack, with the error
+budgets and burn-rate alerts that make paging decisions reviewable. This file
+is the committed source of truth for *what good looks like*; the alert
+transport that delivers a breach is the host assurance lane described in
+[ASSURANCE.md](ASSURANCE.md) and [INCIDENT.md](INCIDENT.md). Metrics and
+lifecycle caveats are in [OBSERVABILITY.md](OBSERVABILITY.md).
+
+## Service level indicators
+
+All ratios are measured over a rolling **30-day** window unless noted. Times
+use UTC. Every series below is emitted by
+`apps/realtime/src/server.ts` (`renderPrometheusMetrics`), scraped from
+`/metrics`, with the fixed label `service="snowride-realtime"`.
+
+| # | SLI | Definition | Source series |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| S1 | Realtime availability | `1 - (5xx responses / all HTTP responses)` | `snowride_realtime_http_responses_total{status_class="5xx"}` vs the sum over `status_class` |
+| S2 | Session-authority integrity | `1 - (replay failures / replay checks)` | `session_failures_total / session_checks_total` |
+| S3 | Control-stream integrity | `1 - (stream mismatches / stream checks)` | `session_stream_failures_total / session_stream_checks_total` |
+| S4 | Event-ledger integrity | `1 - (ledger failures / ledger validations)` | `ledger_failures_total / ledger_validations_total` |
+| S5 | Run-submission durability | `1 - (submission job failures / runs validated)` | `job_failures_by_kind_total{kind="submission"} / runs_validated_total` |
+| S6 | Purchase processing | `1 - (purchase job failures / purchases)` | `job_failures_by_kind_total{kind="purchases"}` vs `snowride_realtime_purchases` |
+| S7 | Realtime latency | HTTP request p95 | capacity lane `/tmp` JSON (`scripts/capacity-regression.mjs`) |
+| S8 | Client frame budget | frame-time p95 by device class | RUM `snowride_client_frame_p95_ms` / `scripts/frame-budget.mjs` |
+| S9 | Client boot | median boot-to-canvas | RUM `snowride_client_boot_ms` (median) |
+
+S2–S4 are correctness guards: a failure is a strong signal that the server
+rejected authoritative state, so their budgets are deliberately tight.
+
+## Objectives and error budgets
+
+| # | Objective | Target | Error budget / window | Breach action |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| S1 | Realtime availability | **99.5%** | 0.5% (~3h 36m) / 30d | page |
+| S2 | Session-authority integrity | **99.5%** | 0.5% / 30d | page |
+| S3 | Control-stream integrity | **99.5%** | 0.5% / 30d | page |
+| S4 | Event-ledger integrity | **99.5%** | 0.5% / 30d | page |
+| S5 | Run-submission durability | **99.0%** | 1.0% / 30d | page |
+| S6 | Purchase processing | **99.0%** | 1.0% / 30d | page |
+| S7 | Realtime latency | **p95 <= 250 ms** and error rate <= 1% | existing capacity gate (`CAP_P95_MS`, `CAP_MAX_ERROR_PCT`) | page (assurance lane exit 13) |
+| S8 | Client frame budget | **p95 <= 60 ms** desktop / **<= 18 ms** low-tier, zero console errors | existing frame gate (`P95_BUDGET_MS`, `LOW_TIER_P95_MS`) | page (assurance lane exit 14) |
+| S9 | Client boot | **median <= 2500 ms** | 5% of days over budget | review |
+
+S7 and S8 targets are not new: they restate the thresholds already enforced by
+`scripts/capacity-regression.mjs` and `scripts/frame-budget.mjs`. S9 is the
+initial proposal for the boot indicator; see Open questions.
+
+## Burn-rate alerts
+
+Alerts are derived from the error budget, not from raw thresholds. For a 30-day
+budget the fast/slow burn pair is the standard multi-window rule:
+
+| Alert | Condition (30d budget) | For | Route |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `SLOLatencyFastBurn` | 14.4x burn (2% of budget) over 1h **and** 1/12 over 5m | 2m | page |
+| `SLOLatencySlowBurn` | 6x burn (5% of budget) over 6h **and** 1/12 over 30m | 15m | page |
+| `SLOIntegrityFailure` | any increase in `classification_failures_total`, `session_failures_total`, `session_stream_failures_total` or `ledger_failures_total` | 0m | page |
+| `SLOBudgetExhausted` | remaining budget <= 0 | — | freeze non-urgent deploys |
+
+Because counters are process-local and reset on restart (see
+[OBSERVABILITY.md](OBSERVABILITY.md)), burn-rate expressions must use
+reset-aware `increase()` over a range, never a raw counter difference across a
+restart. The `SLOIntegrityFailure` rule is intentionally any-increase: S2–S4
+have no acceptable steady-state failure rate.
+
+## Wiring and ownership
+
+The repository does not yet own the alert transport or schedule (finding
+`OBS-P1-001`, tracked separately): assurance detection runs from the host
+crontab and delivers via ntfy, and the capacity/perf lanes already encode the
+S7/S8 thresholds. When the schedule is committed in-repo, the burn-rate rules
+above should be attached to the capacity lane so a breach raises exit 13/14 and
+an ntfy alert. Until then this file is the agreed basis for paging vs.
+non-paging decisions, and the lanes remain the live mechanism.
+
+## Open questions
+
+1. **S9 boot target** (`median <= 2500 ms`) is a first proposal, not a ratified
+   product number. Confirm against the device target matrix
+   (`docs/content/DEVICES_TARGET_MATRIX.md`) and the RUM baseline before
+   treating it as a page.
+2. **RUM coverage.** S8/S9 depend on client RUM samples; if RUM is disabled or
+   sparse, fall back to the host lane's synthetic frame/boot measurements.
+3. **Alert transport** depends on `OBS-P1-001`; do not assume these rules fire
+   automatically until that schedule is committed.
